/**
  @page guides-io-gridfunctions Grid function I/O
  @brief Saving and loading grid functions in Rodin

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-intro Introduction

  Grid functions represent the discrete solution of a finite element problem.
  Their coefficients are interpreted through a finite element space, its
  basis and its mesh. Saving and loading grid functions is essential for:

  - Storing computed solutions
  - Post-processing and visualization
  - Saving field data needed by application-defined restart logic
  - Transferring data between different analyses

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-save Saving grid functions

  @subsection guides-io-gridfunctions-save-mfem MFEM format

  The MFEM format stores grid functions as text files:

  @snippet IO/MFEM.cpp mfem-save

  @subsection guides-io-gridfunctions-save-xdmf XDMF format (recommended)

  For visualization, the XDMF format is recommended. It stores both mesh
  and field data together and can be opened directly in ParaView:

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/IO/XDMF.h>

  // After computing the solution...
  IO::XDMF xdmf("Output");
  xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("solution", u);
  xdmf.write().flush();
  @endcode

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-load Loading grid functions

  @subsection guides-io-gridfunctions-load-mfem From MFEM format

  Loading a grid function from MFEM requires the mesh and finite
  element space already set up:

  @snippet IO/MFEM.cpp mfem-load

  The @ref examples-io-mfem "complete round-trip example" evaluates the loaded
  field at cell centroids and compares it with the prescribed affine field.

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-coverage Format coverage

  Grid function I/O coverage depends on the role of the format:

  | Format | Tested grid function role |
  |--------|---------------------------|
  | MFEM | P0, P1, and H1 finite element grid functions |
  | MEDIT | Vertex-based solution files (`SolAtVertices`) |
  | HDF5 | Raw degree-of-freedom persistence |
  | XDMF | Evaluated visualization attributes |

  MFEM H1 grid functions are tested against fixture files generated by MFEM
  for degrees `K = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6`, for scalar and vector data, and for both
  MFEM vector orderings. P0 scalar grid functions are tested on Point, Segment,
  Triangle, Quadrilateral, Tetrahedron, Hexahedron, and Wedge meshes. P1 and H1
  vector grid functions use the vector finite element spaces provided by Rodin;
  The listed P0 round-trip tests use scalar fields. Other range and format
  combinations follow their loader/printer specialization contracts.

  @subsection guides-io-gridfunctions-precision Preserve mesh coordinates in high-order round trips

  A field is interpreted on its loaded geometry. Text mesh streams need enough
  digits to retain non-binary coordinates such as @f$1/7@f$. The checked
  degree-five scalar/vector example writes the mesh through a stream with
  `max_digits10` precision, then rebuilds the required incidence relations
  before constructing the restored high-order spaces.

  @snippet IO/H1MFEMGridFunction.cpp h1-mfem-mesh

  The example supplies all three vector components, reloads both fields and
  compares their cell-center values with the prescribed polynomials.
  Build `RodinIOH1MFEMGridFunction`; its source is
  @ref IO/H1MFEMGridFunction.cpp.

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-multiple Multiple fields

  Several fields can be registered in one XDMF
  file:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("MultiField");
  auto grid = xdmf.grid();
  grid.setMesh(mesh);
  grid.add("temperature", temperature);
  grid.add("velocity", velocity);
  grid.add("pressure", pressure);
  xdmf.write().flush();
  @endcode

  All fields will be available for selection in ParaView.

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-vector Vector-valued fields

  Vector-valued grid functions are defined on finite element spaces with
  multiple components:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Create a vector P1 space (2 components)
  P1 vh(mesh, 2);
  GridFunction velocity(vh);
  velocity = [](const Geometry::Point& p)
  {
    return Math::Vector<Real>{{ -p.y(), p.x() }};
  };

  IO::XDMF xdmf("VectorField");
  xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("velocity", velocity);
  xdmf.write().flush();
  @endcode

  @section guides-io-gridfunctions-see-also See also

  - @ref guides-io "I/O in Rodin overview"
  - @ref guides-io-xdmf "XDMF format guide"
  - @ref examples-io-index "I/O examples"

  @example IO/H1MFEMGridFunction.cpp
  */
